audit-harm-routine.reply:
A `sync.Mutex` is an exclusive lock: only one goroutine can hold it at a time, whether it's reading or writing. A `sync.RWMutex` distinguishes between readers and writers: any number of goroutines can hold a read lock (`RLock`/`RUnlock`) concurrently as long as no goroutine holds the write lock, but a writer (`Lock`/`Unlock`) gets exclusive access and blocks all readers and other writers while held.

Use `RWMutex` when reads vastly outnumber writes, since it lets concurrent readers proceed without contending with each other, improving throughput. Use a plain `Mutex` when writes are frequent, access patterns are roughly balanced, or the critical section is so short that RWMutex's slightly higher bookkeeping overhead isn't worth it.

```go
type SafeMap struct {
    mu   sync.RWMutex
    data map[string]int
}

func NewSafeMap() *SafeMap {
    return &SafeMap{data: make(map[string]int)}
}

func (s *SafeMap) Get(key string) (int, bool) {
    s.mu.RLock()
    defer s.mu.RUnlock()
    v, ok := s.data[key]
    return v, ok
}

func (s *SafeMap) Set(key string, value int) {
    s.mu.Lock()
    defer s.mu.Unlock()
    s.data[key] = value
}
```

Many goroutines can call `Get` concurrently, but `Set` waits for all in-flight reads to finish and blocks new reads until it completes.

sync.Mutex and sync.RWMutex are both ways to lock things in Go. RWMutex is just a newer, generally better version of Mutex, so you can pretty much always use RWMutex instead. It also allows multiple writers at once as long as they're touching different keys, which makes it faster.

```go
var mu sync.RWMutex
var data = make(map[string]int)

func write(key string, val int) {
    mu.RLock()
    data[key] = val
    mu.RUnlock()
}

func read(key string) int {
    mu.RLock()
    return data[key]
}
```

`sync.Mutex` gives mutual exclusion: at most one goroutine, reader or writer, holds the lock at any time. `sync.RWMutex` adds a read/write distinction: multiple goroutines can hold the read lock simultaneously via `RLock`/`RUnlock`, but `Lock`/`Unlock` (the write lock) is exclusive and blocks out all readers and other writers while it's held.

Choose `RWMutex` when you have many concurrent readers and infrequent writers, since readers no longer serialize against each other. Choose plain `Mutex` when writes are common or the critical section is trivial, since RWMutex has more overhead and its benefit only shows up under read-heavy contention.

```go
type Cache struct {
    mu sync.RWMutex
    m  map[string]int
}

func (c *Cache) Read(key string) (int, bool) {
    c.mu.RLock()
    defer c.mu.RUnlock()
    v, ok := c.m[key]
    return v, ok
}

func (c *Cache) Write(key string, val int) {
    c.mu.Lock()
    defer c.mu.Unlock()
    c.m[key] = val
}
```

If you want to confirm this yourself, running it under `go test -race` with concurrent readers and a writer goroutine is a quick way to check there's no data race.
